159 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E (Loan approval prediction is an important process in the banking and financial sector because banks and financial institutions must carefully evaluate applicants before granting loans. Traditional loan approval methods can be time-consuming and may sometimes produce inconsistent decisions. Machine learning techniques help improve this process by providing faster, automated and data-driven loan approval predictions.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
160 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E (This study worked with a publicly available dataset from Kaggle which represents loan application records from Dream Housing Finance, that contained information on 614 applicants with 13 variables such as gender, marital status, education, income, loan amount, credit history, property area and loan approval status.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
161 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E (Several preprocessing techniques were applied before model development, including handling missing values, encoding categorical variables, feature scaling and exploratory data analysis \(EDA\). Multiple supervised machine learning models were then developed and evaluated, including Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting and Support Vector Machine \(SVM\).)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
162 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E (Model performance was evaluated using Accuracy, F1-Score, ROC-AUC score, confusion matrix analysis, classification reports and ROC curve analysis.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
163 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E (Among all evaluated models, Logistic Regression demonstrated the best overall performance with an accuracy of 86.18%, F1-Score of 0.908 and ROC-AUC score of 0.848. Although Decision Tree achieved a similar accuracy value, Logistic Regression showed better classification balance, stronger discrimination capability and higher interpretability. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
164 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E (Feature importance analysis revealed that Credit_History was the most influential variable affecting loan approval decisions, followed by variables such as property area, marital status, education and income-related features.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
165 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E (The findings obtained from exploratory data analysis were strongly supported by the advanced machine learning analysis results, increasing the reliability and validity of the study. Compared to several previous studies that mainly focused on single models or relied heavily on resampling techniques such as SMOTE, this study provides a broader comparative analysis of multiple machine learning algorithms while demonstrating that strong predictive performance can still be achieved without applying complex balancing techniques when class imbalance is moderate.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
166 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E (Overall, this study demonstrates the practical usefulness of machine learning techniques for automated loan approval prediction. The proposed approach can help financial institutions improve decision-making consistency, reduce manual effort, support financial risk management and provide faster and more reliable loan approval processes.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
167 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 124 0 R
/Pg 10 0 R
